  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "13d07cf1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Define start cell"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "df1c6bec",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Palantir expects that you provide an example cell as a start point. The algorithm will use this cell as the initiating point of the trajectory but with each iteration, the start cell will be refined automatically. In other words, palantir is very robust to the choice of start cell, so it is OK that you only provide an _approximate_ `start_cell`.\n",
    "\n",
    "But how does one generally assign a start cell? This is a context dependent problem. In this data, the authors decided to find the cell with high expression of CD34 gene (the most primitive cell from Cluster 0) and define it as the start point. For this they selected a random cell from the highlighted region below. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 28,
   "id": "b16dd37e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Define a start point\n",
    "# Please note that the start_cell should be the barcode of the cell estimated to be an ideal start point\n",
    "start_cell = 'Run5_164698952452459'"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 29,
   "id": "0aae61e4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1600x600 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "fig = plt.figure(figsize = (8*2, 6))\n",
    "ax = fig.add_subplot(1, 2, 1)\n",
    "select_cells = adata_bm.obs['clusters'] == '0'\n",
    "ax.scatter(adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][:, 0], adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][:, 1], s = 1)\n",
    "ax.scatter(adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][select_cells, 0], adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][select_cells, 1], s = 1)\n",
    "ax.axis('off')\n",
    "ax.set_title('Cluster to select start cell')\n",
    "\n",
    "ax = fig.add_subplot(1, 2, 2)\n",
    "select_cells = adata_bm.obs_names.get_loc(start_cell)\n",
    "ax.scatter(adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][:, 0], adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][:, 1], s = 1)\n",
    "ax.scatter(adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][select_cells, 0], adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][select_cells, 1], s = 100)\n",
    "ax.axis('off')\n",
    "ax.set_title('Selected start cell');"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a8fef2e0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Define terminal states"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7469f698",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Palantir can automatically identify the terminal points, so you can begin your analysis by seeing what palantir identifies as terminal state(s). However, if you want to use biological intuition to identify possible terminal states, you can specify the cells which you think are likely terminal states. \n",
    "\n",
    "If you want a data driven way to identify potential terminal states then you can study the cells with the minimum and maximum values of the diffusion components. Since Palantir builds on the diffusion components, you can study the components itself to identify potential end states. Now, we will color the tSNE by diffusion components, and highlight the cells with min and max values for each component. \n",
    "\n",
    "Note: we only look at the first 8 diffusion component because the gap in eigenvalues is highest there, meaning that palantir will focus on these 8 components to derive a pseudotime."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 30,
   "id": "2ddef0e8",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "(5780, 6)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 30,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm.obsm['DM_EigenVectors_multiscaled'].shape"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 31,
   "id": "7f126e4c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 3200x1200 with 16 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Visualize the first 8 eigenvectors (Note: we ignore the 0th so 7 really)\n",
    "ncol = 4\n",
    "nrow = 2\n",
    "\n",
    "fig = plt.figure(figsize = (8*ncol, 6*nrow))\n",
    "for j in range(8):\n",
    "    ax = fig.add_subplot(nrow, ncol, j + 1)\n",
    "    im1 = ax.scatter(adata_bm.obsm['tsne'][:, 0], adata_bm.obsm['tsne'][:, 1], s = 2, \n",
    "                 c = adata_bm.obsm['DM_EigenVectors'][:, j], cmap = 'plasma')\n",
    "    \n",
    "    [id_min, id_max] = np.argsort(adata_bm.obsm['DM_EigenVectors'][:, j])[[0, -1]]\n",
    "    \n",
    "    ax.scatter(adata_bm.obsm['tsne'][id_min, 0], \n",
    "               adata_bm.obsm['tsne'][id_min, 1], s = 100, c = 'r')\n",
    "    \n",
    "    ax.scatter(adata_bm.obsm['tsne'][id_max, 0], \n",
    "               adata_bm.obsm['tsne'][id_max, 1], s = 100, c = 'k')\n",
    "    \n",
    "    fig.colorbar(im1)\n",
    "    ax.axis('off')\n",
    "    ax.set_title('Diff Map - ' + str(j), fontsize = 16)\n",
    "    \n",
    "# To save: \n",
    "# fig.savefig(output_directory + 'umap_colored_by_diff_maps.png', bbox_inches = 'tight', dpi = 150)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fb1ee53e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In the plot above the tsne representation of the data is colored by each diffusion component. The cells with minimum value for the diffusion component is highlighted in red, while the cells with the maximum value for the diffusion component is highlighted is black. For the plots above, we can speculate that the potential terminal states are as follows after taking care of duplicates:\n",
    "\n",
    "1) min and max of diffusion component 1\n",
    "\n",
    "2) max of diffusion component 2\n",
    "\n",
    "3) max of diffusion component 3\n",
    "\n",
    "4) max of diffusion component 5\n",
    "\n",
    "5) max of diffusion component 6\n",
    "\n",
    "We are making this claim, also based on the gene expression and cell types in each of these terminal states. So, when you adapt this code for your own data, we suggest that you study your data based on gene expression and as above where we looked at the cells with minimum and maximum values for each diffusion component."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "bde5bbe2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Let's get the cell barcode for the cells corresponding to the minimum and maximum values of the above mentioned diffusion components. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 32,
   "id": "a7cae4dc",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "terminal_states = []\n",
    "\n",
    "# for component 1\n",
    "cell_comp1 = np.argsort(adata_bm.obsm['DM_EigenVectors'][:, 1])[[0, -1]]\n",
    "terminal_states = terminal_states + list(adata_bm.obs_names[cell_comp1])\n",
    "\n",
    "# for component 2\n",
    "cell_comp2 = np.argsort(adata_bm.obsm['DM_EigenVectors'][:, 2])[-1]\n",
    "terminal_states.append(adata_bm.obs_names[cell_comp2])\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# for component 3\n",
    "cell_comp3 = np.argsort(adata_bm.obsm['DM_EigenVectors'][:, 3])[-1]\n",
    "terminal_states.append(adata_bm.obs_names[cell_comp3])\n",
    "\n",
    "# for component 5\n",
    "cell_comp5 = np.argsort(adata_bm.obsm['DM_EigenVectors'][:, 5])[-1]\n",
    "terminal_states.append(adata_bm.obs_names[cell_comp5])\n",
    "\n",
    "# for component 6\n",
    "cell_comp6 = np.argsort(adata_bm.obsm['DM_EigenVectors'][:, 6])[-1]\n",
    "terminal_states.append(adata_bm.obs_names[cell_comp6])"
   ]
  },
