  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5a3044b4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now to further process pr_res, we will follow: https://nbviewer.org/github/dpeerlab/Palantir/blob/master/notebooks/Palantir_sample_notebook.ipynb\n",
    "\n",
    "Palantir generates the following results\n",
    "\n",
    "- `palantir_pseudotime`: Pseudo time ordering of each cell\n",
    "- `palantir_fate_probabilities`: Matrix of cells X terminal states. Each entry represents the probability of the corresponding cell reaching the respective terminal state\n",
    "- `palantir_entropy`: A quantiative measure of the differentiation potential of each cell computed as the entropy of the multinomial terminal state probabilities"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d7dc94b0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Get the pseudotime"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "35081460",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm.obs['palantir_pseudotime']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "72f46301",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In the output above, each row is a cell barcode and the number associated with each cell is it's pseudotime alignment score (between 0 representing early and 1 representing differentiated)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "97edbda8",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Get the branch probabilities\n",
    "# As shown below, palantir used the 6 terminally differentiated states\n",
    "adata_bm.obsm['palantir_fate_probabilities']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "763946c2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# You can rename the different branches:\n",
    "adata_bm.obsm['palantir_fate_probabilities'].columns = ['Branch1', 'Branch2', 'Branch3', 'Branch4', 'Branch5', 'Branch6']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "7aa225bd",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Get the entropy\n",
    "adata_bm.obs['palantir_entropy']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e6e8e5ea",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Visualize the pseudotime"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "cf28dbb5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sc.pl.tsne(adata_bm, color = ['palantir_entropy', 'palantir_pseudotime'])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2ad6cb02",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Visualize branch probability\n",
    "nrow = 2\n",
    "ncol = 3\n",
    "fig = plt.figure(figsize = (8*ncol, 6*nrow))\n",
    "for j, item in enumerate(pr_res.branch_probs.columns):\n",
    "    ax = fig.add_subplot(nrow, ncol, j+1)\n",
    "    im1 = ax.scatter(adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][:, 0], adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][:, 1], \n",
    "              c = adata_bm.obsm['palantir_fate_probabilities'][item], s = 2, cmap = 'plasma')\n",
    "    ax.axis('off')\n",
    "    ax.set_title('Branch probability - ' + str(j + 1), fontsize = 16)\n",
    "    fig.colorbar(im1)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f40783b2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Visualize the probability for cells to reach certain terminal state"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "c920d32f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "example_cells = [\"Run5_164698952452459\", \"Run5_170327461775790\", \"Run4_121896095574750\"]\n",
    "fig = plt.figure(figsize = (8*2, 6*2))\n",
    "ax = fig.add_subplot(2, 2, 1)\n",
    "cell_id = [adata_bm.obs_names.get_loc(k) for k in example_cells]\n",
    "ax.scatter(adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][:, 0], adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][:, 1], s = 1)\n",
    "ax.scatter(adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][cell_id[0], 0], adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][cell_id[0], 1], s = 100, \n",
    "          c = 'r', label = example_cells[0])\n",
    "ax.scatter(adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][cell_id[1], 0], adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][cell_id[1], 1], s = 100, \n",
    "          c = 'g', label = example_cells[1])\n",
    "ax.scatter(adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][cell_id[2], 0], adata_bm.obsm['X_tsne'][cell_id[2], 1], s = 100, \n",
    "          c = 'k', label = example_cells[2])\n",
    "ax.axis('off')\n",
    "ax.legend()\n",
    "\n",
    "ax = fig.add_subplot(2, 2, 2)\n",
    "ax.bar(adata_bm.obsm['palantir_fate_probabilities'].columns, \n",
    "       adata_bm.obsm['palantir_fate_probabilities'].loc[example_cells[0]])\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Branches')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Probability')\n",
    "ax.set_title(example_cells[0])\n",
    "\n",
    "ax = fig.add_subplot(2, 2, 3)\n",
    "ax.bar(adata_bm.obsm['palantir_fate_probabilities'].columns, \n",
    "       adata_bm.obsm['palantir_fate_probabilities'].loc[example_cells[1]])\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Branches')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Probability')\n",
    "ax.set_title(example_cells[1])\n",
    "\n",
    "ax = fig.add_subplot(2, 2, 4)\n",
    "ax.bar(adata_bm.obsm['palantir_fate_probabilities'].columns, \n",
    "       adata_bm.obsm['palantir_fate_probabilities'].loc[example_cells[2]])\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Branches')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Probability')\n",
    "ax.set_title(example_cells[2])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7963f9e0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Visualize gene expression trends"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "730e0dc9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Palantir allows us to study the gene expression trends along each of the branches or the pseudotime. This can help us identify regions along the pseudotime when gene expression change substantially, which can be informative about the biology of the system.  "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5851b132",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "First, you can choose a branch to highlight:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b5585630",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "masks = palantir.presults.select_branch_cells(adata_bm, eps=0)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "21f8783f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "65444924",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm.obsm['branch_masks']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1dea20c9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Visualizing the branch selection:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "ebf3fe34",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "help(palantir.plot.plot_branch_selection)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2701c3f7",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "palantir.plot.plot_branch_selection(adata_bm, embedding_basis = 'tsne')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2f67caaf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now we can compute the gene expression trend along the trajectory. It is recommended that you MAGIC impute the data for this otherwise the trends will not be informative (due to 0s)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "bbda840d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import sys\n",
    "sys.path.append(\"/data/peer/sharmar1/msk_workshop/workshop_2024_spring/python_funs/\")\n",
    "import workshop_extra"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "5939098e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "142189fa",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "workshop_extra.run_MAGIC(adata_bm, \n",
    "                         t = 3, \n",
    "                         use_rep= 'X_pca', \n",
    "                         n_components = 30, \n",
    "                         metric = 'euclidean', \n",
    "                         key_added = 'magic', \n",
    "                         impute = True)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "030193e5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "gene_trends = palantir.presults.compute_gene_trends(adata_bm, expression_key=\"magic_imputed_data\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2f125494",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "728abe5d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "genes = [\"CD34\", \"MPO\", \"GATA1\", \"IRF8\"]\n",
    "palantir.plot.plot_gene_trends(adata_bm, genes)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "49eec4fa",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "As heatmaps:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "e3c71058",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "palantir.plot.plot_gene_trend_heatmaps(adata_bm, genes)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "47279936",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Clustering gene expression trends"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "615a4502",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "It may also be informative to group genes together based on how they trend along the pseudotime. For exposition, we will randomly sample 1000 genes from the data and cluster them based on how they trend along Branch1."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "4505f5da",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import random\n",
    "sample_genes = list(random.sample(list(adata_bm.var_names), 1000))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "65f56dc9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "help(palantir.presults.cluster_gene_trends)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "54b69251",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "communities = palantir.presults.cluster_gene_trends(adata_bm, \n",
    "                                                    branch_name = \"Branch1\", \n",
    "                                                    genes = sample_genes)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "5d9d3f5b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "palantir.plot.plot_gene_trend_clusters(adata_bm, \"Branch1\")\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "eb680451",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## PAGA"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2c356215",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "PAGA (PArtition-based Graph Abstraction) is another method that can be used to study connectivities in single-cell data. Often, studying cell differentiation process using pseudotime may not be optimal due to issues resulting from inadequate sampling of cells. In such cases, the notion of connecting clusters of cells using graph-based metrics (to avoid errors that Euclidean distance might cause) has been proposed as an alternative, and PAGA is one such method. PAGA attempts to preserve both continuous and disconnected structure in the data. \n",
    "\n",
    "Ref: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6425583/"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d1ff5c9e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "PAGA relies on both clustering and nearest neighbor graphs (which can be constructed on PCA or on diffusion components). We will provide code to do both."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "f1396e2a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "911b75ec",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sc.pl.tsne(adata_bm, color = 'clusters')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "1e637907",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# We are computing 300 principal components as that is what the authors used. \n",
    "# The rationale was that they wanted to consider enough PCs to explain 75% of the variance in the data\n",
    "sc.tl.pca(adata_bm) # , n_comps = 300)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "80c967ac",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Compute nearest neighbors \n",
    "sc.pp.neighbors(adata_bm, n_neighbors=30, use_rep = 'X_pca', metric='euclidean', key_added='neighbors_30')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "72f949f4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### PAGA with distances computed on PCA"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "83d3dbaf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Since we have already computed k-nearest neighbor distance graph on the PCA, we can simply invoke that result."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "cd4426d1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sc.tl.paga(adata_bm, groups='clusters', neighbors_key = 'neighbors_30')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "5a11d105",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "fd3e1619",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm.uns['paga']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "244c1745",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sc.pl.paga(adata_bm, color=['clusters'], node_size_scale = 2)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5858bf8e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Interpreting the plot above:\n",
    "1. Each circle indicates a cluster and the size of the circle is proportional to size of the cluster.\n",
    "2. Two circles that are phenotypically close to each other - as quantified by the distances computed on the PCA space - are connected to each other. \n",
    "3. The width of the connecting edges is proportional to the similarity between the clusters.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "We can also highlight the expression of select genes onto the PAGA plot as follows:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "975384cd",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sc.pl.paga(adata_bm, color = ['CD34', 'MPO', 'GATA1'], node_size_scale = 2)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "08f06b23",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### PAGA on diffusion components"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "35e0dde0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "As we discussed above, PAGA can also compute connectivites between clusters based on distances in the diffusion space. For the example, below we will consider distances in the diffusion space defined by the first seven eigenvectors (ignoring the 0th eigenvector -- automatically ignored by PAGA)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "55b942d5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2d01331c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sc.tl.diffmap(adata_bm, n_comps=30, neighbors_key = 'neighbors_30')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "26e0bf3c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "plt.scatter(range(len(adata_bm.uns['diffmap_evals'])), adata_bm.uns['diffmap_evals'])\n",
    "plt.xlabel('Index')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('EigenValue')\n",
    "plt.grid('on')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "773e921b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Based on the above plot, we will consider only the top 7 components:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "dd628357",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm.obsm['diffmap_evecs'] = adata_bm.obsm['X_diffmap'][:, 1:8]\n",
    "adata_bm.uns['diffmap_evals'] = adata_bm.uns['diffmap_evals'][1:8]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "0193701a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm.obsm['diffmap_evecs'].shape"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b7b4dcca",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Compute neighbors in diffusion components space:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "4f30f849",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sc.pp.neighbors(adata_bm, n_neighbors=30, use_rep='diffmap_evecs', key_added = 'diff_neighbors')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fe616c2d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Re-do PAGA on diffusion map space:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "843a3b51",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sc.tl.paga(adata_bm, groups='clusters', neighbors_key = 'diff_neighbors')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "0525cccb",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "feef4b02",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sc.pl.paga(adata_bm, node_size_scale = 2)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8d238f0e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "As you can see the two results of PAGA look visually quite similar (except that cluster 9 was more connected to other clusters when computing distances in the PCA space vs when computing in the diffusion space). It will be up to you to evaluate such cases using what is known about the cell types. In this case, Cluster 9 is ITGA2B+ (Megakaryocytes) and it is more phenotypically closer to cluster 2 and 8 as opposed to clusters 1 and 4. Therefore, diffusion distance based PAGA would be more faithful representation of the data here. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "245f353b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Using results to quantify distance between clusters"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1b0b7230",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "You can extract the similarity between clusters using diffusion maps space as follows. We see 0 when two clusters are not connected to each other in PAGA."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "e8541356",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df = pd.DataFrame(adata_bm.uns['paga']['connectivities'].toarray(),\n",
    "             index = ['Cluster-' + str(j) for j in np.unique(adata_bm.obs['clusters'])], \n",
    "             columns = ['Cluster-' + str(j) for j in np.unique(adata_bm.obs['clusters'])])\n",
    "\n",
    "df"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4b6c7091",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Note: We can always repurpose this code to define distance or similarity between any two groups of cells by just replacing `clusters` column in obs with whatever grouping of cells we want."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e66bb9a1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Alternatively, for data where cells are along a continuum, we can use the diffusion components to compute phenotypic distances between cells or clusters. As we discussed, Euclidean distance in the diffusion component space is a good approximation for phenotypic distance between cells. Of course, we need to be careful with only the meaningful diffusion components (e.g. in the above case we will use diffusion components 1-7). \n",
    "\n",
    "In the following code, we will compute distance (A) between each pair of cells within cluster 0 (B) between cells in cluster 0 and cluster 1. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "f8d887b3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "e5a165df",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata_bm.obsm['diffmap_evecs'].shape"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "4c683e94",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "cells_C0 = np.where(adata_bm.obs['clusters'] == '0')[0]\n",
    "cells_C1 = np.where(adata_bm.obs['clusters'] == '1')[0]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "96593245",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(len(cells_C0), len(cells_C1))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "0419bcf0",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.metrics import pairwise_distances\n",
    "distance_pairwise_C0 = pairwise_distances(adata_bm.obsm['diffmap_evecs'][cells_C0, :])\n",
    "distance_pairwise_C0_C1 = pairwise_distances(adata_bm.obsm['diffmap_evecs'][cells_C0, :], \n",
    "                                             adata_bm.obsm['diffmap_evecs'][cells_C1, :])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "28303c99",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(distance_pairwise_C0.shape, distance_pairwise_C0_C1.shape)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ca7ac7c3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "As we see, `distance_pairwise_C0` holds the pairwise distance between cells in cluster 0; `distance_pairwise_C0_C1` holds the pairwise distance between cells in cluster 0 and cluster 1."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0935da6f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can visualize the distances as a boxplot for comparison. Also, note that `distance_pairwise_C0` is a symmetric matrix as distance from cell1 in cluster 0 to cell2 in cluster 0 is the same as distance from cell2 in cluster0 to cell1 in cluster 0. So we will only look at the upper traingle of the `distance_pairwise_C0` matrix."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "29584f75",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "within_C0 = distance_pairwise_C0[np.triu_indices(distance_pairwise_C0.shape[0], k = 1)]\n",
    "between_C0_C1 = distance_pairwise_C0_C1.flatten()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "151dee9a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "plt.boxplot([within_C0, between_C0_C1]);\n",
    "plt.xticks([1, 2], ['Distance inside C0', 'Distance between C0 and C1']);\n",
    "plt.ylabel('Distance in diffusion space')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "6b92915e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": []
  }
 ],
